            "author": "iamwillcannon",
            "region": "US",
            "shares": 9,
            "caption": "50 AI agents were told to build a civilization in Minecraft. After 22 deaths on the first night, they got so careful they hid in shelters. It’s called Civ50, a 3-hour experiment by Y Combinator Labs and Olam Labs. Each agent was a separate AI (OpenAI’s GPT-6.1 Sol) that could see the game, make its own choices and message the others. No human controlled them. In 90 seconds, they cut down almost every tree near spawn. Nobody voted for a leader, but 46 of the other 49 agents messaged an agent named Ada first, because her name was at the top of the list. Then night came. Monsters caused 22 of the run’s 34 deaths. Dying only cost them the gear they carried, but the agents got more and more careful. They wrote rules for everything: a food ration list, fishing shifts and job titles. 4 agents spent 11 minutes appointing a “sole fertilizer operator” for a single piece of bone meal on one wheat block. After the third night, Ada told everyone to stop optional building and far scouting and wait in shelters. The researchers say it felt like patterns they’ve seen in AI coding agents. @ycombinator @olam_labs #AI #AIAgents",
